    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "A new study of folding, faulting, and fracturing of the Upper Cretaceous Cerro Toro Formation consisting of deep-water clastics of the Magallanes Foreland Basin in Chilean Patagonia has provided several intriguing results: The course-grained units appear to control the folding of the Silla Syncline, with a wavelength of about 4 to 8 km, while the fine-grained units control the E-W trending fold and thrust belts within the shoulder of the Silla Syncline. The folds associated with these belts have shorter wavelengths but steeper limbs. One of the thrust fault zones has a documented dip slip of about 30 m and contains slip surfaces, fragmented and brecciated rock, and calcite precipitants. The fault zones, however, lack evidence for ductile smearing. Interplay between the deposition of the course-grained materials and the regional/local tectonic structures is possible, but remains to be constrained.\r\n\r\nThe entire study area has been deformed by an apparent conjugate system of N70°E and N80°W strike- slip faults. The former has right lateral slip with the largest measured value of about 40 m, whereas the latter has left lateral slip with the largest measured value of about 5 m. The mechanism of the faulting is inferred to be brittle jointing and subsequent shearing of cross joints. The lack of localized deformation bands in the granular turbidities is intriguing and remains to be further explored.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
